{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793305950,"nonce":"HX46cV2gphy1mTacJ69Mv3TqRUg=","message":"Native LayerCommand::Meshes records consecutive compatible scene snapshots in their represented group. Camera/viewport, paint coverage and every primitive/path/image/command cursor gate scene merging, so intervening 2D output or callbacks preserve ordering. Captures share mesh assets rather than copying vertices/indices. The common callback coverage replacement helper now also runs mesh scenes through the original physical frame.\n\nScene dispatch and camera/light matrix helpers moved from app context into a bounded GPU renderer module. Both grouped and independently queued output use it. Camera/viewport runs have separate HDR/depth/shadow/tone-map boundaries; invalid viewports and empty geometry are filtered before batch numbering. Consecutive compatible instances keep opaque-before-blend sorting, shared shadows and one Scene3D hook execution. No shader, GPU primitive/instance, FFI or stable-ID changes.\n\nNine new offscreen cases pass normally: /private/tmp/blinc-mesh-group-matrix-final.log. Coverage includes the recorded opacity/order negative, snapshot/direct/compositor replay, foreground owners, effects and opacity endpoints, DPI1/2 and 1x/4x, shared depth/shadows and transparent ordering, path/explicit opacity, shapes/RGBA/callback scene barriers, empty sentinels and multiple independent viewports, live binding zero recovery, one scene-hook execution with a glass neighbor, and cropped named output. Early fixture delimiter and geometry-tone thresholds were corrected before the runtime negative. The later barrier fixture had LayerConfig::new's zero opacity; it was corrected without a production change.\n\nGrouped scenes use the callback path's two temporary exact frame-sized RGBA textures, about 8*w*h bytes in RGBA8, plus existing group/effect bakes and the existing HDR/depth/OIT/shadow resources. Compatible instances share those edits per scene rather than per mesh. Glass shares a prepared background. Pool policy is unchanged; there is no CPU readback or new geometry upload policy. No timing, peak-memory, browser or mobile claim. Mesh canvas viewport interpretation remains the existing axis-aligned API; no new arbitrary affine or unsupported 3D ancestor claim.\n\nThe host command enum gains Meshes, requiring exhaustive Rust matches to update in release API review. take_pending_meshes now drains only ungrouped instances. External-GPU batches rebake when painted instead of caching opaque scene state. Scoped lint, adjacent rendering controls and DT/restored probes are running. No broad gates, desktop input, push or publication.","files":null}]}